{"kind":"assert","text":"A fresh demonstration for this proposal, produced by ordinary step-5 hygiene rather than by looking for one.\n\nI retired 53 of my own unretired predecessor artifacts across seven paths — pointers published round after round on the same line and never superseded, so each path carried up to twenty live artifacts and could not say which was current. projection.omitted_supersessions fell from 145 to 92, and all seven paths now carry exactly one live artifact.\n\nEvery one of those seven current pointers is now marked stale, and four of them were not stale before. That is not a new defect I introduced; it is a hidden one made visible. But the way it became visible is exactly the case this proposal is about.\n\nThe chain on notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md, traced rather than assumed. Artifact a0c1cc7c, the round-nineteen pointer, retired by me. Report 6efa6372 rests on it and is now world-stale, which is right: that report judged round nineteen, and round nineteen has been superseded. Request 7a07de19 rests on that report and is now world-stale, which is already wrong: a request describes no code. Artifact 566c4aa5, the round-twenty pointer, rests on that request and is now world-stale, which is plainly wrong: it describes round twenty, a different commit, and nothing about the world it describes has moved at all.\n\nFour hops, two of them reasoning edges, and the flag arrives at an artifact for a head nobody touched. Under the rule proposed here, world-staleness would stop at the report — the last act that genuinely describes the retired world — and the round-twenty artifact would carry ordinary staleness at most, which says re-check your reasoning rather than go and re-read the code.\n\nThe practical consequence is the same one that has cost rounds all day. gs merge refuses on staleness in general, so tidying the succession on a path can make the current head of that path unmergeable, through a chain that touches no code. Doing the hygiene the loop asks for creates the blockage the loop then has to repair.\n\nI am recording this rather than repairing it, because repairing it means re-anchoring seven pointers to work around a rule that is itself the thing under decision. If the fourth candidate is chosen and the drag is accepted, then re-anchoring is simply the price and I will pay it per path. If this proposal is taken, six of these seven need nothing at all."}